'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}]",Its good,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': Hi}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Hey Anthony, it\'s good to see you. How are you feeling today? I\'m here to listen and help you work through anything that’s on your mind.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its been caothic. I barely slept today. Three alarms, hit snooze every time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it sounds like the chaos has really taken a toll on you today, especially with the sleep disruption. When you\'re feeling this overwhelmed, what’s one small thing that might help ground you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I dont know. My body feels wired. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that wired feeling is your body\'s way of signaling it needs to come back to calm. Let\'s try a quick 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, then exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds. Would you be willing to try that with me right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Great, Anthony - let\'s do that together right here, right now. Inhale... 4 seconds. Hold... 7 seconds. Exhale... 8 seconds. How does that feel for your ""wired"" sensation?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I\'m glad you tried it. Can you tell me a bit more about what ""wired"" feels like in your body right now? Any specific sensations or thoughts that might be fueling this feeling?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'My mind keeps running in the background - replaying work issues, things i said to my partner, deadlines, its like im constantly buffering.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that mental buffering you\'re describing is actually your mind trying to protect you from feeling overwhelmed - it\'s like your brain is stuck in emergency mode. Since you noticed the breathing helped with the wired feeling, could we explore how to gently ""pause"" that buffer when it gets too loud?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, let\'s try something simple: when you notice that mental buffer kicking in, pause for just a second and gently label the thought you\'re having - like ""work stress"" or ""partner concern"" - then let it float by without following the train of thought. What if we practiced this together for just two minutes right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Ok}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, when you\'re ready, could you try noticing one thought that comes up right now and gently naming it? It doesn\'t have to be anything big - just noticing is the first step. What would that feel like for you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im worried}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That ""wired"" feeling you described earlier often shows up as worry when we\'re trying to sleep. Anthony, would you be willing to try a 30-second grounding exercise right now? Just notice three things you can see, two you can touch, and one you can hear - it\'s like hitting pause on the worry loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. But im feeling anxious. The less i sleep, the more anxious i get, which makes it even harder to sleep. its this feedback loop i cant break, and then i get exhausted.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that sleep-anxiety cycle is incredibly draining, and I\'m glad you\'re recognizing it. When exhaustion makes it hard to focus, let\'s try a simpler breathing pattern: just 4 seconds in, 4 seconds hold, 4 seconds out - repeat three times. It\'s like whispering to your nervous system, ""I\'m safe right now."" What if we tried that together for 30 seconds?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im trying}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I notice you\'re really trying — and it\'s completely normal for the mind to wander when you\'re tired. Let\'s gently bring your attention back to the breath for just a moment. How does it feel to notice the air moving in and out of your body right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s wonderful to hear, Anthony. When you\'re in that exhausted state, even a few seconds of calm can feel like a lifeline. What if we used these small moments of relief as stepping stones to gradually break that sleep-anxiety cycle?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.'}]","Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': Hi}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Hey Anthony, it\'s good to see you. How are you feeling today? I\'m here to listen and help you work through anything that’s on your mind.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its been caothic. I barely slept today. Three alarms, hit snooze every time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it sounds like the chaos has really taken a toll on you today, especially with the sleep disruption. When you\'re feeling this overwhelmed, what’s one small thing that might help ground you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I dont know. My body feels wired. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that wired feeling is your body\'s way of signaling it needs to come back to calm. Let\'s try a quick 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, then exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds. Would you be willing to try that with me right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Great, Anthony - let\'s do that together right here, right now. Inhale... 4 seconds. Hold... 7 seconds. Exhale... 8 seconds. How does that feel for your ""wired"" sensation?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I\'m glad you tried it. Can you tell me a bit more about what ""wired"" feels like in your body right now? Any specific sensations or thoughts that might be fueling this feeling?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'My mind keeps running in the background - replaying work issues, things i said to my partner, deadlines, its like im constantly buffering.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that mental buffering you\'re describing is actually your mind trying to protect you from feeling overwhelmed - it\'s like your brain is stuck in emergency mode. Since you noticed the breathing helped with the wired feeling, could we explore how to gently ""pause"" that buffer when it gets too loud?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, let\'s try something simple: when you notice that mental buffer kicking in, pause for just a second and gently label the thought you\'re having - like ""work stress"" or ""partner concern"" - then let it float by without following the train of thought. What if we practiced this together for just two minutes right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Ok}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, when you\'re ready, could you try noticing one thought that comes up right now and gently naming it? It doesn\'t have to be anything big - just noticing is the first step. What would that feel like for you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im worried}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That ""wired"" feeling you described earlier often shows up as worry when we\'re trying to sleep. Anthony, would you be willing to try a 30-second grounding exercise right now? Just notice three things you can see, two you can touch, and one you can hear - it\'s like hitting pause on the worry loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. But im feeling anxious. The less i sleep, the more anxious i get, which makes it even harder to sleep. its this feedback loop i cant break, and then i get exhausted.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that sleep-anxiety cycle is incredibly draining, and I\'m glad you\'re recognizing it. When exhaustion makes it hard to focus, let\'s try a simpler breathing pattern: just 4 seconds in, 4 seconds hold, 4 seconds out - repeat three times. It\'s like whispering to your nervous system, ""I\'m safe right now."" What if we tried that together for 30 seconds?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im trying}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I notice you\'re really trying — and it\'s completely normal for the mind to wander when you\'re tired. Let\'s gently bring your attention back to the breath for just a moment. How does it feel to notice the air moving in and out of your body right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s wonderful to hear, Anthony. When you\'re in that exhausted state, even a few seconds of calm can feel like a lifeline. What if we used these small moments of relief as stepping stones to gradually break that sleep-anxiety cycle?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That chest tightness is such a clear signal your body\'s carrying a lot, Anthony. It makes sense that even exercise feels different when your nervous system\'s stuck in high gear. What if we tried something tiny for now - like just 2 minutes of stretching while noticing how your breath moves through that tightness? It might help your body remember how to relax.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. I just keep thinking: i should be able to handle this. Other people have more stressful jobs and lifes.'}]",I can try. I just keep thinking: i should be able to handle this. Other people have more stressful jobs and lifes.,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': Hi}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Hey Anthony, it\'s good to see you. How are you feeling today? I\'m here to listen and help you work through anything that’s on your mind.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its been caothic. I barely slept today. Three alarms, hit snooze every time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it sounds like the chaos has really taken a toll on you today, especially with the sleep disruption. When you\'re feeling this overwhelmed, what’s one small thing that might help ground you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I dont know. My body feels wired. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that wired feeling is your body\'s way of signaling it needs to come back to calm. Let\'s try a quick 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, then exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds. Would you be willing to try that with me right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Great, Anthony - let\'s do that together right here, right now. Inhale... 4 seconds. Hold... 7 seconds. Exhale... 8 seconds. How does that feel for your ""wired"" sensation?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I\'m glad you tried it. Can you tell me a bit more about what ""wired"" feels like in your body right now? Any specific sensations or thoughts that might be fueling this feeling?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'My mind keeps running in the background - replaying work issues, things i said to my partner, deadlines, its like im constantly buffering.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that mental buffering you\'re describing is actually your mind trying to protect you from feeling overwhelmed - it\'s like your brain is stuck in emergency mode. Since you noticed the breathing helped with the wired feeling, could we explore how to gently ""pause"" that buffer when it gets too loud?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, let\'s try something simple: when you notice that mental buffer kicking in, pause for just a second and gently label the thought you\'re having - like ""work stress"" or ""partner concern"" - then let it float by without following the train of thought. What if we practiced this together for just two minutes right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Ok}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, when you\'re ready, could you try noticing one thought that comes up right now and gently naming it? It doesn\'t have to be anything big - just noticing is the first step. What would that feel like for you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im worried}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That ""wired"" feeling you described earlier often shows up as worry when we\'re trying to sleep. Anthony, would you be willing to try a 30-second grounding exercise right now? Just notice three things you can see, two you can touch, and one you can hear - it\'s like hitting pause on the worry loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. But im feeling anxious. The less i sleep, the more anxious i get, which makes it even harder to sleep. its this feedback loop i cant break, and then i get exhausted.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that sleep-anxiety cycle is incredibly draining, and I\'m glad you\'re recognizing it. When exhaustion makes it hard to focus, let\'s try a simpler breathing pattern: just 4 seconds in, 4 seconds hold, 4 seconds out - repeat three times. It\'s like whispering to your nervous system, ""I\'m safe right now."" What if we tried that together for 30 seconds?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im trying}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I notice you\'re really trying — and it\'s completely normal for the mind to wander when you\'re tired. Let\'s gently bring your attention back to the breath for just a moment. How does it feel to notice the air moving in and out of your body right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s wonderful to hear, Anthony. When you\'re in that exhausted state, even a few seconds of calm can feel like a lifeline. What if we used these small moments of relief as stepping stones to gradually break that sleep-anxiety cycle?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That chest tightness is such a clear signal your body\'s carrying a lot, Anthony. It makes sense that even exercise feels different when your nervous system\'s stuck in high gear. What if we tried something tiny for now - like just 2 minutes of stretching while noticing how your breath moves through that tightness? It might help your body remember how to relax.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. I just keep thinking: i should be able to handle this. Other people have more stressful jobs and lifes.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that ""should"" thinking is really common when we\'re struggling - it\'s like we expect ourselves to handle things differently than we actually can right now. I wonder if you could try noticing how your body feels in this stretch compared to how you imagine others might feel? Maybe we could explore what your body needs in this moment rather than comparing.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I think i feel worse. I keep thinking about worst-case scenarios - at work, my relationshp, things like the dog getting sick or my parents dying suddenly. its constant.'}]","I think i feel worse. I keep thinking about worst-case scenarios - at work, my relationshp, things like the dog getting sick or my parents dying suddenly. its constant.",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': Hi}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Hey Anthony, it\'s good to see you. How are you feeling today? I\'m here to listen and help you work through anything that’s on your mind.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its been caothic. I barely slept today. Three alarms, hit snooze every time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it sounds like the chaos has really taken a toll on you today, especially with the sleep disruption. When you\'re feeling this overwhelmed, what’s one small thing that might help ground you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I dont know. My body feels wired. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that wired feeling is your body\'s way of signaling it needs to come back to calm. Let\'s try a quick 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, then exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds. Would you be willing to try that with me right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Great, Anthony - let\'s do that together right here, right now. Inhale... 4 seconds. Hold... 7 seconds. Exhale... 8 seconds. How does that feel for your ""wired"" sensation?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I\'m glad you tried it. Can you tell me a bit more about what ""wired"" feels like in your body right now? Any specific sensations or thoughts that might be fueling this feeling?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'My mind keeps running in the background - replaying work issues, things i said to my partner, deadlines, its like im constantly buffering.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that mental buffering you\'re describing is actually your mind trying to protect you from feeling overwhelmed - it\'s like your brain is stuck in emergency mode. Since you noticed the breathing helped with the wired feeling, could we explore how to gently ""pause"" that buffer when it gets too loud?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, let\'s try something simple: when you notice that mental buffer kicking in, pause for just a second and gently label the thought you\'re having - like ""work stress"" or ""partner concern"" - then let it float by without following the train of thought. What if we practiced this together for just two minutes right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Ok}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, when you\'re ready, could you try noticing one thought that comes up right now and gently naming it? It doesn\'t have to be anything big - just noticing is the first step. What would that feel like for you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im worried}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That ""wired"" feeling you described earlier often shows up as worry when we\'re trying to sleep. Anthony, would you be willing to try a 30-second grounding exercise right now? Just notice three things you can see, two you can touch, and one you can hear - it\'s like hitting pause on the worry loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. But im feeling anxious. The less i sleep, the more anxious i get, which makes it even harder to sleep. its this feedback loop i cant break, and then i get exhausted.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that sleep-anxiety cycle is incredibly draining, and I\'m glad you\'re recognizing it. When exhaustion makes it hard to focus, let\'s try a simpler breathing pattern: just 4 seconds in, 4 seconds hold, 4 seconds out - repeat three times. It\'s like whispering to your nervous system, ""I\'m safe right now."" What if we tried that together for 30 seconds?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im trying}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I notice you\'re really trying — and it\'s completely normal for the mind to wander when you\'re tired. Let\'s gently bring your attention back to the breath for just a moment. How does it feel to notice the air moving in and out of your body right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s wonderful to hear, Anthony. When you\'re in that exhausted state, even a few seconds of calm can feel like a lifeline. What if we used these small moments of relief as stepping stones to gradually break that sleep-anxiety cycle?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That chest tightness is such a clear signal your body\'s carrying a lot, Anthony. It makes sense that even exercise feels different when your nervous system\'s stuck in high gear. What if we tried something tiny for now - like just 2 minutes of stretching while noticing how your breath moves through that tightness? It might help your body remember how to relax.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. I just keep thinking: i should be able to handle this. Other people have more stressful jobs and lifes.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that ""should"" thinking is really common when we\'re struggling - it\'s like we expect ourselves to handle things differently than we actually can right now. I wonder if you could try noticing how your body feels in this stretch compared to how you imagine others might feel? Maybe we could explore what your body needs in this moment rather than comparing.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I think i feel worse. I keep thinking about worst-case scenarios - at work, my relationshp, things like the dog getting sick or my parents dying suddenly. its constant.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it\'s completely understandable to have these thoughts when anxiety is high - your mind\'s trying to protect you from potential threats. I\'m wondering if you could notice how these thoughts show up in your body right now? Like, does your chest feel tighter or your breathing shallower when these worst-case scenarios come up?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'M body feels tight. It starts with a worry , then it spirals. I picture myself getting fired, losing the apartment, ending up alone, with no partner or family. It sounds overly dramatic, but it feels real...'}]","M body feels tight. It starts with a worry , then it spirals. I picture myself getting fired, losing the apartment, ending up alone, with no partner or family. It sounds overly dramatic, but it feels real...",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': Hi}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Hey Anthony, it\'s good to see you. How are you feeling today? I\'m here to listen and help you work through anything that’s on your mind.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its been caothic. I barely slept today. Three alarms, hit snooze every time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it sounds like the chaos has really taken a toll on you today, especially with the sleep disruption. When you\'re feeling this overwhelmed, what’s one small thing that might help ground you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I dont know. My body feels wired. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that wired feeling is your body\'s way of signaling it needs to come back to calm. Let\'s try a quick 4-7-8 breathing technique: inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, then exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds. Would you be willing to try that with me right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Great, Anthony - let\'s do that together right here, right now. Inhale... 4 seconds. Hold... 7 seconds. Exhale... 8 seconds. How does that feel for your ""wired"" sensation?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes. Ill try that}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I\'m glad you tried it. Can you tell me a bit more about what ""wired"" feels like in your body right now? Any specific sensations or thoughts that might be fueling this feeling?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'My mind keeps running in the background - replaying work issues, things i said to my partner, deadlines, its like im constantly buffering.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that mental buffering you\'re describing is actually your mind trying to protect you from feeling overwhelmed - it\'s like your brain is stuck in emergency mode. Since you noticed the breathing helped with the wired feeling, could we explore how to gently ""pause"" that buffer when it gets too loud?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yes}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, let\'s try something simple: when you notice that mental buffer kicking in, pause for just a second and gently label the thought you\'re having - like ""work stress"" or ""partner concern"" - then let it float by without following the train of thought. What if we practiced this together for just two minutes right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Ok}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, when you\'re ready, could you try noticing one thought that comes up right now and gently naming it? It doesn\'t have to be anything big - just noticing is the first step. What would that feel like for you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im worried}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That ""wired"" feeling you described earlier often shows up as worry when we\'re trying to sleep. Anthony, would you be willing to try a 30-second grounding exercise right now? Just notice three things you can see, two you can touch, and one you can hear - it\'s like hitting pause on the worry loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. But im feeling anxious. The less i sleep, the more anxious i get, which makes it even harder to sleep. its this feedback loop i cant break, and then i get exhausted.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that sleep-anxiety cycle is incredibly draining, and I\'m glad you\'re recognizing it. When exhaustion makes it hard to focus, let\'s try a simpler breathing pattern: just 4 seconds in, 4 seconds hold, 4 seconds out - repeat three times. It\'s like whispering to your nervous system, ""I\'m safe right now."" What if we tried that together for 30 seconds?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Im trying}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I notice you\'re really trying — and it\'s completely normal for the mind to wander when you\'re tired. Let\'s gently bring your attention back to the breath for just a moment. How does it feel to notice the air moving in and out of your body right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Its good}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s wonderful to hear, Anthony. When you\'re in that exhausted state, even a few seconds of calm can feel like a lifeline. What if we used these small moments of relief as stepping stones to gradually break that sleep-anxiety cycle?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes. I need it. My chest feels tight most of the day. Ive started skipping workouts, wich doesnt help. But even when i do exercise, its not the same. i used to feel better after, but now i just feel tired.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That chest tightness is such a clear signal your body\'s carrying a lot, Anthony. It makes sense that even exercise feels different when your nervous system\'s stuck in high gear. What if we tried something tiny for now - like just 2 minutes of stretching while noticing how your breath moves through that tightness? It might help your body remember how to relax.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I can try. I just keep thinking: i should be able to handle this. Other people have more stressful jobs and lifes.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, that ""should"" thinking is really common when we\'re struggling - it\'s like we expect ourselves to handle things differently than we actually can right now. I wonder if you could try noticing how your body feels in this stretch compared to how you imagine others might feel? Maybe we could explore what your body needs in this moment rather than comparing.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I think i feel worse. I keep thinking about worst-case scenarios - at work, my relationshp, things like the dog getting sick or my parents dying suddenly. its constant.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, it\'s completely understandable to have these thoughts when anxiety is high - your mind\'s trying to protect you from potential threats. I\'m wondering if you could notice how these thoughts show up in your body right now? Like, does your chest feel tighter or your breathing shallower when these worst-case scenarios come up?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'M body feels tight. It starts with a worry , then it spirals. I picture myself getting fired, losing the apartment, ending up alone, with no partner or family. It sounds overly dramatic, but it feels real...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Anthony, I hear how real these scenarios feel in your body and mind, even if they seem extreme. What if we gently noticed where in your body you feel the ""wired"" sensation when these thoughts spiral? Sometimes noticing the physical anchors can help us step out of the mental loop.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'In my chest. It gets so bad that i just... freeze. Ill be staring at my screen, trying to code, and my hands are literally shaking.'}]","In my chest. It gets so bad that i just... freeze. Ill be staring at my screen, trying to code, and my hands are literally shaking.",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
