nderstandable - sometimes we notice patterns in ourselves only when someone else points them out. What comes to mind when you think about those early morning hours - what are you usually thinking or feeling during that time?'}, {'role': user, 'content': Sometimes I replay old legal cases and think about what I could have done better. Some of it I reflect on my life now and how I feel stagnant. Some I just lay there with a blank mind}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'It makes sense that your mind might replay work-related thoughts - that\'s the pattern you\'ve lived with for so long. But when these thoughts come up at 3am, what do they actually do for you? Do they feel like they\'re helping you make sense of things, or just adding to that sense of being stuck?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I\'m not sure, maybe a bit of both'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Edward, it\'s really common for our minds to get stuck in these loops when we\'re trying to make sense of things - especially when work has been such a big part of our identity. What if we thought about those 3am moments not as failures, but as your mind\'s way of trying to find meaning? How might we create a small boundary between your work identity and your rest time?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I\'m not so sure I\'m good at that. Can you give me some suggestions?'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Edward, I\'d love to suggest a few small shifts that might help create a clearer boundary between work thoughts and rest. What if we tried one of these:  
