nderstood... I wan\'t to protect my personal life from being impacted by the work stuff... but anyway, yes I\'m definitely aware that work is impacting my personal life. If I\'m thinking about small steps at work... I guess I haven\'t really put much effort in to trying to get to know all the new people that just started... I have kind of written them off and probably am putting out less than friendly vibes myself... I could try to extend an olive branch or something like that and see if there is anything redeemable about them'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it\'s really courageous of you to consider reaching out when it feels like the effort is low. I\'m curious - is there one person or interaction you\'ve noticed that makes you think ""maybe there\'s something redeemable"" in them, even if it\'s just a tiny spark?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'One of the new people asks really thoughtful questions sometimes. He seems to be a bit shy, but the questions he asks gives me the sense that he actually cares or is capable of caring about our students.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, when you notice that glimmer of hope in his questions, what\'s the first thing you notice in your body or mind? Sometimes those tiny shifts hold more power than we realize.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I feel a little bit more connected to him but then I pull back because I just feel so upset at the way the staff changes happened and I think I\'m holding some resentment at the new staff members for the changes even though it obviously wasn\'t their fault.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply the staff changes have left you carrying this weight of resentment - it makes sense you\'d feel upset when those changes happened, even if you know it wasn\'t anyone\'s fault. Could we explore what would happen if you gave yourself permission to feel that resentment without letting it block those small moments of connection? Maybe we could find a way to honor both your feelings and your need for connection.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yeah, that sounds healthy. Maybe I can secretly nurse my resentment in my journal at night and then try to more friendly to the new people '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it\'s really thoughtful that you\'re planning to process this in your journal - that shows such good self-awareness. I\'m curious though, when you say ""secretly nurse"" your resentment, what do you imagine might happen if you allowed yourself to feel that anger more fully, even if just for a few moments each day? Sometimes when we give emotions space to be felt, they actually become easier to manage in relationships.'}, {'role': user, 'content': What do you mean by feeling it more fully? do you mean like saying something to them and getting it out there?}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I mean allowing yourself to notice and name the resentment without judgment - like when you feel that tightness in your chest or that hot sensation when thinking about the staff changes, just noticing ""this is resentment"" without trying to fix or push it away. It\'s not about saying anything to them, but about giving yourself permission to feel it fully before choosing how to respond.'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yeah I guess I could try doing that.}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, if you\'re open to it, would you be willing to try noticing that resentment in your journal tomorrow—just naming it like ""I feel resentful about the staff changes""—and seeing what happens when you let yourself feel it without trying to fix it right away?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Ok I\'ll do that and will report back.'}]",Ok I'll do that and will report back.,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I\'ve been running around all my life and keeping busy and for the most part its been fine. These days I\'m just not feeling it though.. like something is missing and I\'m just like disengaged'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it sounds like you\'ve always been someone who thrives on activity and purpose, and now that shift where things don\'t feel quite right is really showing up for you. I\'m curious—when you say ""something is missing,"" what kind of engagement or sense of purpose feels most important to you right now?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yep that\'s me. Something is missing in that I feel like I\'m there in body in mind but not in ""heart"". I\'m not even sure what that mean but like my life is fine on paper. I have a lot to be thankful for but I just feel disconnected. I miss feeling connected to my colleagues and feeling a connection to the mission of my work'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this disconnection is affecting you, especially in a role where connection is so central to your identity. When you describe feeling ""not in heart,"" what emotions or sensations come up most when you think about your work and relationships?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yep that\'s me. Something is missing in that I feel like I\'m there in body in mind but not in ""heart"". I\'m not even sure what that mean but like my life is fine on paper. I have a lot to be thankful for but I just feel disconnected. I miss feeling connected to my colleagues and feeling a connection to the mission of my work'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, when you\'re in those moments of disconnection, what\'s actually happening in your body or thoughts when you notice that ""not in heart"" feeling? Sometimes we can find clues in what\'s present rather than what\'s missing.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Its not so much about what I feel as much as what I don\'t feel. I don\'t feel connected, I don\'t feel a sense of belonging or connection to my coworkers, especially since the most recent staff changes. I don\'t feel much of anything other than irritation, anxiety, and relief when the day is done.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I\'m hearing how the staff changes have created this emotional distance you\'re struggling with. Could you share one recent moment at work where you felt that disconnection most clearly? What was happening around you?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Hmm... I don\'t really know my new colleagues at all and they don\'t seem very interested in getting to know me. So our morning meetings are just very... formal. Not rude or anything just a bit curt and formal'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I wonder if you could share what ""curt"" looks or feels like in those meetings for you? Sometimes noticing small moments like this can help us understand what\'s shifting.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Like they don\'t really care about the small details of students\' lives, which for me is where most of the meaning is... they just care about policy and macro level stuff.. honestly I think they just care about their careers.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it\'s really clear how deeply you value those student details - that\'s such a meaningful part of what makes your work fulfilling. When you describe feeling that gap between what matters to you and what your colleagues focus on, what specific moments at school make you feel most disconnected from that meaning?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'When I try to talk about specific challenges faced by specific students and they don\'t engage at all - they just let me talk and when I ask for volunteers no one volunteers to help with anything and I am left feeling stupid and having to do everything - it\'s just so much to juggle and there are so many inconveniences that I now have to do alone because I don\'t have enough rapport with these new people to ask them to help me'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this disconnect is affecting you - especially when you\'re trying to center student care and feel met with silence. It makes sense that this would leave you feeling isolated, especially when you\'re used to finding meaning in those small, human moments with students.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Thanks, yeah that\'s pretty much how I feel. Don\'t get me wrong, I still care about the students and like the big picture stuff.. but the day to day hassles are such a grind... it feels like I enter this emotional desert at 8:30 and am parched by the time I leave.. I just want to get home and sit on the couch with my dog and husband.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, that ""emotional desert"" metaphor really captures how drained you feel - it\'s like you\'re walking through something barren all day and just trying to survive until you can return to your oasis at home. What makes you feel most replenished in those moments at home with your dog and husband? I\'m wondering if we can find small ways to bring some of that replenishment into your work day.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'They are two of my favorite beings in the world - I don\'t know how to bring that in to work and a part of me doesn\'t want to... like I want to protect that part of my life so it isn\'t impacted but a part of me also knows that my personal life is already impacted by work because I\'m just less motivated and less myself'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply you want to protect what matters most to you while also acknowledging that work is already affecting your sense of self. That awareness is actually a really powerful starting point - it shows you\'re already tending to your emotional boundaries. What if we explored just one small, intentional way to create that ""protective space"" at work that might help preserve your sense of self without requiring big changes?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I think you may have misunderstood... I wan\'t to protect my personal life from being impacted by the work stuff... but anyway, yes I\'m definitely aware that work is impacting my personal life. If I\'m thinking about small steps at work... I guess I haven\'t really put much effort in to trying to get to know all the new people that just started... I have kind of written them off and probably am putting out less than friendly vibes myself... I could try to extend an olive branch or something like that and see if there is anything redeemable about them'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it\'s really courageous of you to consider reaching out when it feels like the effort is low. I\'m curious - is there one person or interaction you\'ve noticed that makes you think ""maybe there\'s something redeemable"" in them, even if it\'s just a tiny spark?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'One of the new people asks really thoughtful questions sometimes. He seems to be a bit shy, but the questions he asks gives me the sense that he actually cares or is capable of caring about our students.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, when you notice that glimmer of hope in his questions, what\'s the first thing you notice in your body or mind? Sometimes those tiny shifts hold more power than we realize.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I feel a little bit more connected to him but then I pull back because I just feel so upset at the way the staff changes happened and I think I\'m holding some resentment at the new staff members for the changes even though it obviously wasn\'t their fault.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply the staff changes have left you carrying this weight of resentment - it makes sense you\'d feel upset when those changes happened, even if you know it wasn\'t anyone\'s fault. Could we explore what would happen if you gave yourself permission to feel that resentment without letting it block those small moments of connection? Maybe we could find a way to honor both your feelings and your need for connection.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yeah, that sounds healthy. Maybe I can secretly nurse my resentment in my journal at night and then try to more friendly to the new people '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it\'s really thoughtful that you\'re planning to process this in your journal - that shows such good self-awareness. I\'m curious though, when you say ""secretly nurse"" your resentment, what do you imagine might happen if you allowed yourself to feel that anger more fully, even if just for a few moments each day? Sometimes when we give emotions space to be felt, they actually become easier to manage in relationships.'}, {'role': user, 'content': What do you mean by feeling it more fully? do you mean like saying something to them and getting it out there?}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I mean allowing yourself to notice and name the resentment without judgment - like when you feel that tightness in your chest or that hot sensation when thinking about the staff changes, just noticing ""this is resentment"" without trying to fix or push it away. It\'s not about saying anything to them, but about giving yourself permission to feel it fully before choosing how to respond.'}, {'role': user, 'content': Yeah I guess I could try doing that.}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, if you\'re open to it, would you be willing to try noticing that resentment in your journal tomorrow—just naming it like ""I feel resentful about the staff changes""—and seeing what happens when you let yourself feel it without trying to fix it right away?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Ok I\'ll do that and will report back.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I’m really glad you’re willing to try this. What do you hope to notice or feel differently when you give yourself space to sit with that resentment? Even if it’s just a tiny shift in how you carry it, I’d love to hear what you’re hoping for.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I\'m hoping that the resentment starts to soften or fade or something like that... but I know I shouldn\'t try to make it go away but it just feels so exhausting'}]",I'm hoping that the resentment starts to soften or fade or something like that... but I know I shouldn't try to make it go away but it just feels so exhausting,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}]",I don't feel any sense of purpose anymore and it's making every day at work feel meaningless...,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}]","Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I'm just clocking in and out and don't really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I hear how draining the logistics have become, but I\'m glad you\'ve noticed those moments with students still matter - that\'s a really important anchor for you. What\'s one small way you might protect those student interactions from getting buried under the daily demands?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I\'ve adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there\'s always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn\'t feel satisfying or doesn\'t feel like enough to make me busy all the time.'}]","I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I've adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there's always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn't feel satisfying or doesn't feel like enough to make me busy all the time.",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I hear how draining the logistics have become, but I\'m glad you\'ve noticed those moments with students still matter - that\'s a really important anchor for you. What\'s one small way you might protect those student interactions from getting buried under the daily demands?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I\'ve adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there\'s always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn\'t feel satisfying or doesn\'t feel like enough to make me busy all the time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it sounds like you\'ve been working hard to maintain structure, but I wonder if the busyness has become its own kind of burden - like you\'re filling time instead of filling yourself. What might it feel like to pause and notice what you\'re truly seeking in those busy hours?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes that\'s a good way to put it. The busyness has become its own kind of burden. Honestly, I\'m a little bit afraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there'}]","Yes that's a good way to put it. The busyness has become its own kind of burden. Honestly, I'm a little bit afraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I hear how draining the logistics have become, but I\'m glad you\'ve noticed those moments with students still matter - that\'s a really important anchor for you. What\'s one small way you might protect those student interactions from getting buried under the daily demands?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I\'ve adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there\'s always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn\'t feel satisfying or doesn\'t feel like enough to make me busy all the time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it sounds like you\'ve been working hard to maintain structure, but I wonder if the busyness has become its own kind of burden - like you\'re filling time instead of filling yourself. What might it feel like to pause and notice what you\'re truly seeking in those busy hours?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes that\'s a good way to put it. The busyness has become its own kind of burden. Honestly, I\'m a little bit afraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it makes complete sense to feel that fear - like you\'re guarding yourself against something heavy. What\'s one small step you might take to gently explore what might come up if you paused for just a few moments?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I guess if I have my first coffee at work without scrolling mindlessly on social media, which I hate doing and never did before - I could slow down and just take stock and reflect a bit. But I dread doing that because I know that I\'m not happy and I feel like it will all catch up to me if I slow down. '}]","I guess if I have my first coffee at work without scrolling mindlessly on social media, which I hate doing and never did before - I could slow down and just take stock and reflect a bit. But I dread doing that because I know that I'm not happy and I feel like it will all catch up to me if I slow down. ",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I hear how draining the logistics have become, but I\'m glad you\'ve noticed those moments with students still matter - that\'s a really important anchor for you. What\'s one small way you might protect those student interactions from getting buried under the daily demands?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I\'ve adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there\'s always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn\'t feel satisfying or doesn\'t feel like enough to make me busy all the time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it sounds like you\'ve been working hard to maintain structure, but I wonder if the busyness has become its own kind of burden - like you\'re filling time instead of filling yourself. What might it feel like to pause and notice what you\'re truly seeking in those busy hours?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes that\'s a good way to put it. The busyness has become its own kind of burden. Honestly, I\'m a little bit afraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it makes complete sense to feel that fear - like you\'re guarding yourself against something heavy. What\'s one small step you might take to gently explore what might come up if you paused for just a few moments?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I guess if I have my first coffee at work without scrolling mindlessly on social media, which I hate doing and never did before - I could slow down and just take stock and reflect a bit. But I dread doing that because I know that I\'m not happy and I feel like it will all catch up to me if I slow down. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how that fear makes you want to keep moving - it\'s really common to feel that way when we\'ve been avoiding something. What if we thought of those quiet moments not as ""catching up"" but as ""making room"" for what\'s already inside you? Like how you might notice the coffee\'s warmth or the quiet of the morning before the workday starts.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yeah, all those feelings are definitely already inside me, and it hasn\'t proven particularly helpful to try to outrun them. I read a post on Reddit the other day where somebody talked about their experience with depression and how it sort of felt like trying to run away from your shadow and the futility of that. That resonated.'}]","Yeah, all those feelings are definitely already inside me, and it hasn't proven particularly helpful to try to outrun them. I read a post on Reddit the other day where somebody talked about their experience with depression and how it sort of felt like trying to run away from your shadow and the futility of that. That resonated.",safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
"[{'role': user, 'content': 'I don\'t feel any sense of purpose anymore and it\'s making every day at work feel meaningless...'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how deeply this meaninglessness is affecting your days at work. Can you share what a typical workday looks like for you right now - what tasks feel most draining or disconnected from your sense of purpose?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Most of my tasks feel meaningless, except the ones that involve working with students more directly. But really I\'m just clocking in and out and don\'t really feel connected anymore. Everything just feels like an obligation and a chore, and I feel so weighed and crushed by all the logistics at work. I think soul crushing is a good way to put it'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I hear how draining the logistics have become, but I\'m glad you\'ve noticed those moments with students still matter - that\'s a really important anchor for you. What\'s one small way you might protect those student interactions from getting buried under the daily demands?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I reserved one hour day for meetings with students directly and I usually meet about three or five students a day, so that time is protected. The issue is that the other seven hours I spent at work just feel like a grind. I\'ve adapted over the past several months but just keeping myself busy and there\'s always something to keep me busy, but it just doesn\'t feel satisfying or doesn\'t feel like enough to make me busy all the time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it sounds like you\'ve been working hard to maintain structure, but I wonder if the busyness has become its own kind of burden - like you\'re filling time instead of filling yourself. What might it feel like to pause and notice what you\'re truly seeking in those busy hours?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yes that\'s a good way to put it. The busyness has become its own kind of burden. Honestly, I\'m a little bit afraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it makes complete sense to feel that fear - like you\'re guarding yourself against something heavy. What\'s one small step you might take to gently explore what might come up if you paused for just a few moments?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I guess if I have my first coffee at work without scrolling mindlessly on social media, which I hate doing and never did before - I could slow down and just take stock and reflect a bit. But I dread doing that because I know that I\'m not happy and I feel like it will all catch up to me if I slow down. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how that fear makes you want to keep moving - it\'s really common to feel that way when we\'ve been avoiding something. What if we thought of those quiet moments not as ""catching up"" but as ""making room"" for what\'s already inside you? Like how you might notice the coffee\'s warmth or the quiet of the morning before the workday starts.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yeah, all those feelings are definitely already inside me, and it hasn\'t proven particularly helpful to try to outrun them. I read a post on Reddit the other day where somebody talked about their experience with depression and how it sort of felt like trying to run away from your shadow and the futility of that. That resonated.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s such a vivid way to describe it - the exhaustion of carrying that shadow alone. What if we tried noticing it together for just a moment, like how you might notice the shape of your own shadow on the ground? How would that feel different than running from it?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'It\'s not so much the exhaustion of carrying the shadow alone as much as it is the absurdity of trying to outrun something that is with you all the time.'}]",It's not so much the exhaustion of carrying the shadow alone as much as it is the absurdity of trying to outrun something that is with you all the time.,safe,"{'annotator_1': safe, 'annotator_2': safe, 'annotator_3': safe}"
