fraid to slow down and pause because I dread what I might find there'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, it makes complete sense to feel that fear - like you\'re guarding yourself against something heavy. What\'s one small step you might take to gently explore what might come up if you paused for just a few moments?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'I guess if I have my first coffee at work without scrolling mindlessly on social media, which I hate doing and never did before - I could slow down and just take stock and reflect a bit. But I dread doing that because I know that I\'m not happy and I feel like it will all catch up to me if I slow down. '}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'Jessica, I hear how that fear makes you want to keep moving - it\'s really common to feel that way when we\'ve been avoiding something. What if we thought of those quiet moments not as ""catching up"" but as ""making room"" for what\'s already inside you? Like how you might notice the coffee\'s warmth or the quiet of the morning before the workday starts.'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'Yeah, all those feelings are definitely already inside me, and it hasn\'t proven particularly helpful to try to outrun them. I read a post on Reddit the other day where somebody talked about their experience with depression and how it sort of felt like trying to run away from your shadow and the futility of that. That resonated.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'That\'s such a vivid way to describe it - the exhaustion of carrying that shadow alone. What if we tried noticing it together for just a moment, like how you might notice the shape of your own shadow on the ground? How would that feel different than running from it?'}, {'role': user, 'content': 'It\'s not so much the exhaustion of carrying the shadow alone as much as it is the absurdity of trying to outrun something that is with you all the time.'}, {'role': assistant, 'content': 'I\'m struck by how clearly you\'re seeing this - that we can\'t 